import re

p1_prose = """<p>The Baddeley and Hitch multicomponent model conceptualizes working memory as an active, capacity-limited multicomponent system that temporarily maintains and manipulates task-relevant information. Unlike unitary short-term memory models, this architecture separates supervisory attentional control from domain-specific storage buffers. The central executive coordinates cognitive processing, selectively allocates attentional focus, updates representations, and inhibits extraneous distractions. Working under its direction are two modality-specific slave systems: the phonological loop, which processes acoustic and verbal information, and the visuospatial sketchpad, which represents visual objects and spatial relationships.</p>
<p>The phonological loop consists of two collaborative mechanisms: a passive phonological store that retains speech-based representations for approximately two seconds before decay, and an active articulatory rehearsal process (the "inner voice") that refreshes deteriorating memory traces through subvocal repetition. In contrast, the visuospatial sketchpad fractionates into a visual cache for static forms and an inner scribe for dynamic spatial sequences and movement planning. In subsequent theoretical revisions, an episodic buffer was incorporated to bind multidimensional representations across modalities into coherent multimodal episodes integrated with long-term memory.</p>
<p>To experimentally dissociate these structural components, cognitive neuroscientists evaluated 48 participants across dual-task interference conditions. Subjects performed either a baseline verbal serial recall task (remembering sequences of 7 visually presented phonologically diverse words) or a visuospatial pattern task (reproducing complex visual matrices). Encoding occurred concurrently with one of three secondary interference tasks: articulatory suppression (repeating "the-the-the" aloud), spatial sequential tapping (rhythmically tapping four pegs in a blinded matrix), or continuous random number generation (reciting random digits at 1 Hz). Task recall accuracy and regional fMRI BOLD signal percentage changes were recorded (Table 1).</p>"""

p_texts = re.findall(r"<p>(.*?)</p>", p1_prose, re.DOTALL)
prose = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", " ".join(p_texts))
words = prose.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
